From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 23:43:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] download: fail a post-message that is gone from the
 server
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A download-on-demand message whose post-message the server no longer has
spun forever instead of failing: no percentage, no failure, no retry
button. A chatmail relay expiring the message is enough to reach this.

The failure decision in `download_msgs()` is keyed on `available_post_msgs`,
but that flag never survives for a message the user can tap:
`download_known_post_messages_without_pre_message()` runs right before
`download_msgs()` on every cycle and drops every entry whose pre-message is
already in the database (`rfc724_mid_exists` matches the pre-message stub).
So every error took the "will be downloaded once it arrives" branch, kept
the queue row, and retried forever. When the relay had expunged the
message, the chunked path's up-front `uid_fetch_meta` probe bailed before
anything else ran, and the stale `imap` row sent the next cycle straight
back to it.

Proof that the message is gone now comes from the relay instead of the
flag. `forget_imap_location()` runs where the chunked path learns that a
relay does not serve the UID anymore — the up-front probe finds nothing or
finds it `\Deleted`, or a later chunk comes back empty and the re-probe
confirms it — and deletes that relay's row. If another relay still has a
location, the message stays queued and the inboxes are interrupted, so that
relay downloads it now, from scratch (staging files are per transport). If
none is left, the message is set to `Failure` and dequeued, which puts the
retry button back.

`download_msg()`'s "no location known on any transport" branch now fails
the message too, instead of waiting. Waiting is only right for a
post-message that has not been seen yet, which cannot be told apart from
one that is gone, and without this a retry after a proven failure would
spin forever again. Tapping download in the seconds between a pre-message
and its post-message arriving now fails once; the retry button fetches it.

The location lookup is now `imap_location()`, named because its
transport-spanning behavior is load-bearing: only a message no relay has a
location for may be failed. Tests cover the lookup across transports and
the forget decision with two relays, without involving the flag.

The whole-message fallback (servers without partial FETCH) cannot tell
"gone" from "fetch failed" and leaves its row alone.

Also fixes the chunked path leaving `InProgress` behind when `receive_imf`
rejects the assembled message: the device message was posted and the
queue entry dropped, but nothing moved the message out of `InProgress`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
